\fBNAME\fR
.br
.in 4n
usilic - pager and file manager
.in 0
.sp
\fBSYNOPSIS\fR
.br
.in 4n
usilic [-c|-g] [-f font] [-s usec ...] [-d dot ...]
.br
         [file] [< file ...] [directory]
.in 0
.sp
\fBDESCRIPTION\fR
.br
.in 4n
usilic works as a pager when started with standard input or regular files as arguments, and as a file manager when given a directory argument. After startup, the arguments can be checked on the first line ("OS name : command line") of the \fBINFO\fR screen shown by ^I (hold control and press "I"), but the very first start always opens as a pager in the \fBHELP\fR \fBmode\fR, whatever the arguments. If required programs need to be installed, it switches to \fBSHELL\fR meanwhile and returns to \fBHELP\fR when it finishes (during the install, keys other than typing characters, ^H and ^M do nothing; in an emergency, quit with ^[^[^M).
.br
.sp
From \fBHELP\fR, ^[s, ^J., ^J, and ^Js (after the install, also ^G on the chapter list) move to a limited \fBSHELL\fR (prompt \fBUSILIC\fR, only commands such as ls), and ^G returns to \fBHELP\fR. Quit once with q and start again, and the usual screen of usilic appears. From then on, start with -H, or press ^T^H after startup, to open \fBHELP\fR, and -K or ^T^K opens \fBKEY\fR (all the key assignments), in another window, so try working while referring to the main functions and their usage.
.br
.sp
In a terminal that supports the mouse, scrolling by dragging the screen and with the wheel can be used. In addition, the left/right cursor keys start automatic scrolling, after which the up/down cursor keys adjust its speed.
.br
.sp
Note that in this manual page opened in \fBMAN\fR \fBmode\fR, moving to the previous / next chapter with the ; / : keys (defined in \fBMACRO\fR \fBmode\fR below) and following the notes shown underlined cannot be used.
.br
.sp
The main notations used in the text of \fBHELP\fR are as follows.
